  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{7}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{7+8}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$",
